well there weren't many new  anti-terrorism powers announced by the  prime minister but then he did say he  wanted the uk government's response to  the emerging threat from the terror  group islamic state in iraq and syria to  be considered and not knee-jerk however  there are a few things in this  announcement which will be welcomed by  the police and the security services in  the uk  requiring all airlines who operate out  of the uk to provide the authorities  here with advanced passenger information  can be extremely helpful in the efforts  to determine who might be planning to  travel overseas for the purposes of  terrorism an advanced list of names can  be checked against terror watch lists  and other databases  enhanced powers to seize suspects  passports will also be welcomed by the  authorities in the uk although there are  powers under the royal prerogative to  confiscate someone's passport it is a  fairly cumbersome power and it can't be  applied in real time by police and  border officials who might be suspicious  about a specific passenger  just before they're about to embark on a  flight that new power will enable them  to do that  but with around 500 british nationals  who've already traveled to syria and  iraq there remain significant concerns  about what happens on the return the  government is actively looking at  introducing targeted discretionary  powers to enable the uk to exclude  british nationals from returning to the  uk if it's believed they pose a threat  however this is a difficult prospect  both ethically and legally such a power  would effectively render any uk national  excluded from returning to the country  stateless now that law would undoubtedly  be challenged in the courts and getting  the liberal democrats on board seems  unlikely  so at this stage it's difficult to see  how the government will be able to enact  that final measure
 

